## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of augmented binary search trees to support order statistics—a fundamental data structures concept.

## Key Concepts

- **Augmented BST/Treap**: Each node stores value, count (for duplicates), and subtreeSize
- **Order statistics**: getKthSmallest navigates using left subtree size and node counts
- **Duplicate handling**: Multiple identical values stored in a single node with a count field
- **Rebalancing**: Operations maintain O(log n) time via rotations or split/merge

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(v) {
    this.v = v;
    this.c = 1;      // count of this value
    this.sz = 1;     // subtree size
    this.l = null;
    this.r = null;
    this.p = null;  // priority for treap
  }

  upd() {
    this.sz = (this.l?.sz || 0) + (this.r?.sz || 0) + this.c;
  }
}

function kth(node, k) {
  if (!node) return null;
  const leftSize = node.l?.sz || 0;
  if (k <= leftSize) return kth(node.l, k);
  if (k <= leftSize + node.c) return node.v;
  return kth(node.r, k - leftSize - node.c);
}
```

## Time Complexity

- Insert: O(log n)
- EraseOne: O(log n)
- GetKthSmallest: O(log n)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of order-statistics structures, coordinate compression, and practical trade-offs for large domains in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree (BIT) for prefix sums
- Coordinate compression to handle sparse domains
- kth-order statistics via prefix sums
- Merge strategy: small-to-large to reduce work

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n) {
    this.n = n;
    this.f = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  }
  add(i, delta) {
    for (; i <= this.n; i += i & -i) this.f[i] += delta;
  }
  sum(i) {
    let s = 0;
    for (; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += this.f[i];
    return s;
  }
  kth(k) {
    let idx = 0;
    let bit = 1;
    while ((bit << 1) <= this.n) bit <<= 1;
    for (let d = bit; d > 0; d >>= 1) {
      const next = idx + d;
      if (next <= this.n && this.f[next] < k) {
        idx = next;
        k -= this.f[next];
      }
    }
    return idx + 1;
  }
}
```

